          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "European and US guidelines do differ significantly on when to initiate medication for stage 1 hypertension.\n\n### US Guidelines:\nIn the United States, stage 1 hypertension is defined as a blood pressure reading of 130-139 mm Hg systolic or 80-89 mm Hg diastolic. The US guidelines recommend starting drug treatment alongside lifestyle changes in cases where patients have stage 1 hypertension and other risk factors, such as established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or higher. For those without these features, drug treatment is not immediately indicated; instead, lifestyle measures are prioritized, with reassessment after three to six months before considering medication (Source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\").\n\n### European Guidelines:\nThe European guidelines categorize hypertension differently, defining it as beginning at 140/90 mm Hg. This means that the range considered \"stage 1\" in the US (130-139/80-89 mm Hg) is often classified as 'high-normal' blood pressure in Europe. Consequently, individuals who might be prescribed medication in the US could be advised to pursue lifestyle changes alone in Europe, highlighting a more conservative approach regarding low-risk stage 1 hypertension management (Source: \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\").\n\n### Summary of Differences:\n- **Diagnostic Thresholds**: The US uses 130/80 mm Hg, while Europe uses 140/90 mm Hg.\n- **Initiation of Medication**: The US may start medication more readily in stage 1 hypertension cases with certain risk factors, whereas Europe may prioritize lifestyle changes for those categorized as high-normal blood pressure. \n\nThis variation can lead to differing populations labeled as hypertensive and which patients receive medication (Source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"; \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
